{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to reverse its pioneering approach to drug decriminalization, as a new law takes effect, making it a crime once again to possess small amounts of hard drugs. This new law gives individuals caught with illicit substances the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe treatment program, also known as a \"deflection program,\" aims to divert people from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. However, the law does not mandate counties to create these treatment alternatives, which may lead to inconsistent policies across different regions. Supporters of the law argue that this approach allows counties to develop programs tailored to their resources.\n\n*** \nThe criteria for eligibility to the deflection program are strict, requiring individuals to have no other charges, warrants, or history of violent behavior, and to be medically stable. If an individual meets these requirements, law enforcement officers will connect them with a mobile behavioral health outreach team, which must make contact within 30 minutes. If this timeframe is not met, the individual will be taken to jail.\n\n*** \nThe new law introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas, such as parks, and makes personal-use possession a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. P.P.S As people of all age groups navigate the complexities of this new law, they may find it challenging to eat away at the underlying issues driving drug use in their country, where shopping for solutions can be a daunting task, and the words used to describe the problem can be as sharp as a tongue that speaks truth to power.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.004175516